WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.453 --> 00:00:05.870
ANNIE JEAN-BAPTISTE:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:08.380
I'm Annie Jean-Baptiste,
and I lead product inclusion

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:09.370
at Google.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:12.340
Product inclusion is all about
bringing an inclusive lens

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.050
throughout the product
design process,

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.210
and I'm excited to share
a little bit about how

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.580
we've been doing that.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.920
First, I just want to
welcome you to the session.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.680
This may be a newer
concept for you,

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.305
and so I hope that it's helpful.

00:00:25.305 --> 00:00:26.680
But I'm super
excited that you're

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.090
spending the time to learn
about product inclusion today.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:34.080
Before we get started, I
just wanted to level set

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.570
and have a shared framework
around what we mean

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.700
when we say product inclusion.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.420
Product inclusion is about
building, designing, coding

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.680
for everyone.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.870
So no matter who you are, where
you live, what you look like,

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.620
we want to make sure
that you feel thought

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.810
of in the entire
product design process

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.185
and when you pick up
a product or service.

00:00:54.185 --> 00:00:55.560
And so at Google,
we want to make

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.290
sure that we're bringing
that inclusive lens

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:02.510
throughout everything
that we create.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.319
So the first thing that's
really important to think about

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:09.100
is really, how this ties
back into Google's mission.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.980
And so we talk about organizing
the world's information

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.937
and making it universally
accessible and useful.

00:01:14.937 --> 00:01:17.020
It's really the universal
piece that we're leaning

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.580
into in product inclusion.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.430
And we say not just
for some, for everyone.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.920
So I talked a little bit
about some dimensions

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.100
that we think of, but it's
really important to make sure

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.542
that everyone is thought
of in the process

00:01:29.542 --> 00:01:31.000
and especially,
those who have been

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.800
historically underrepresented
in the product design process.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.800
Now this work builds on a
lot of great work, namely

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.010
accessibility work and
Next Billion Users work.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:43.510
What happens with
product inclusion

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:46.900
is that we actually look at
12 dimensions of diversity

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.463
and the intersections
of those dimensions.

00:01:49.463 --> 00:01:51.130
What I mean when I
say the intersections

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:55.080
of those dimensions is that
users aren't in a silo.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.560
They don't have one thing
that makes them who they are.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.102
And so it's really
important to think

00:01:59.102 --> 00:02:01.330
about everything
holistically when you're

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.370
building products or services.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:06.070
Using myself as an
example, I'm a black woman.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.840
I'm also left-handed.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.240
I'm not black on Monday,
a woman on Tuesday,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:11.890
and left-handed on Wednesday.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.420
All of those things
are encompassed

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.700
within me, affect how I
move through the world,

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.210
and definitely affect
how I use products.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.710
And so we want to make sure
that we're thinking holistically

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:24.290
about all of those dimensions
when we're building.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.513
So on this slide, you're
seeing a few of the dimensions

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:28.180
that we look at.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:29.650
Again, we look at 12.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:33.230
But things like gender,
age, ability, education,

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.950
socioeconomic status,
and more are really

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.310
integral into making sure
that you're asking, who else?

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:42.830
And really broadening who your
users could and should be.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.590
It's also really
important to note

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:46.960
that there's a lot of
untapped opportunity

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.690
when you bring an inclusive
lens and really center

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.630
those who have been
historically underrepresented.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.460
So what you're
seeing on this slide

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.270
are a bunch of statistics around
things like purchasing power,

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:01.780
digital usage for a
lot of the historically

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.620
underrepresented
groups that we focus on

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:04.900
with product inclusion.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.650
So you can see stats as it
relates to accessibility.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.770
There 1 billion people in
the world with a disability--

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:12.850
for example.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.980
Or you can look at 700
million users coming

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.720
online in the next few years.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.180
It's really exciting that
there's so much opportunity

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.010
when you bring an
inclusive lens,

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:22.780
and that can really
affect your business

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.540
and being able to
serve even more users.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.870
It's important to note that
product inclusion is not just

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.430
one point in your process.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.230
It's really throughout
the process.

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.570
And the earlier that you
bring it in, the better.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:36.795
So you can bring
it in an ideation,

00:03:36.795 --> 00:03:38.170
for example, when
you're starting

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:40.810
to think of a product
or service that may

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.490
have an untapped opportunity.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:45.010
But what's really important
is to highlight and understand

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:46.960
what key points in
the process you're

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.570
going to bring that
inclusive lens.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.780
We call those inflection points.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.910
And on this slide, you can see
there are multiple inflection

00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:57.640
points where we started to
bring that inclusive lens

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:58.840
throughout the process.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:01.600
And the goal is that
the end result is

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.770
more inclusive of more people.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.350
So next, I'm just going to
jump into a few case studies

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:11.100
to help you understand what our
product inclusion journey looks

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.260
like, and how this can
actually be put into action.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.100
So this first video
is from a team

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.630
that worked on camera sensors.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.310
And what they wanted
to do was really

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.100
ensure that they brought
this inclusive lens

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.310
and ensured all skin tones
were accurately represented.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:29.400
What I love about this is that
it shows that no matter where

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.440
you are in the organization,
you have a part

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.092
to play in building for
everyone and with everyone.

00:04:34.092 --> 00:04:35.550
So with that, we'll
roll the video.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:35.850
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:36.475
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:38.230
- Pictures tell stories.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.200
It doesn't really
matter if it's a selfie

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.850
or if it's a portrait
that somebody

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.950
is taking of another person.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.740
You are sharing in
that human moment.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.360
- take a picture of you two.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:53.906
1, 2, 3.

00:04:53.906 --> 00:04:55.800
No, he's too dark.

00:04:59.275 --> 00:05:01.170
- So this is where the
discussion started.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:01.795
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:01.795 --> 00:05:03.090
- Here, just use my phone.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.106
It's got a better sensor.

00:05:05.106 --> 00:05:06.750
- A better sensor?

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:07.942
What does that even mean?

00:05:07.942 --> 00:05:11.170
- Color chaining is an
extremely complicated process.

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:13.440
When we look at images,
what we try to do

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.420
is figure out how
much of a difference

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.150
there is between, let's
say, a reference image

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.810
and the image that we actually
are trying to quantify.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.230
We were running some
tests on a product,

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.840
and it was the proximity
sensor we were testing.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.720
And we said, oh, it looks
like it does 60 centimeters.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.360
And we looked to each other,
and we go, we're both white.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.980
The technology itself, as many
people will say, is not racist.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.410
It's just that it
wasn't tested properly

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:45.480
to make sure that the designers
weren't unconsciously biased.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.250
Hey, there's an entire
world out there,

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.374
and we want to make sure
this works for everybody.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.950
I love the fact that we
get to influence cameras.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:56.780
And we get to share
those experiences

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.020
of life and emotion.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.350
And, to me, that is
what imaging is about.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.000
It as a vehicle to
express humanity.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.160
- Take a picture of you two.

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:09.245
1, 2, 3.

00:06:09.245 --> 00:06:14.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.403
[END PLAYBACK]

00:06:15.403 --> 00:06:17.070
ANNIE JEAN-BAPTISTE:
The next case study

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:20.010
I'd like to show you is
with Duo Low Light Mode.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.200
What Duo Low Light
team did was be

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:24.720
intentional about bringing
diverse perspectives

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.050
to the table from the
beginning of their process.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.630
At Google, we have what we
call inclusion champions, who

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.900
are Googlers from across the
globe in multiple dimensions

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.690
of diversity, who have
helped us dog food

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.650
or user test our products.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.280
So the Duo Low
Light mode team knew

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.590
that they wanted to make
sure that no matter where

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:47.100
you were in the world or
if you had certain lighting

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:49.628
or you had light
or dark skin tones,

00:06:49.628 --> 00:06:51.420
you were going to be
accurately represented

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.770
when using this video product.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:56.070
And so, they worked with
our inclusion champions

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:58.620
to make sure that we tested
across multiple dimensions

00:06:58.620 --> 00:06:59.693
of diversity.

00:06:59.693 --> 00:07:01.860
To make sure that, again,
everyone feels beautifully

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.510
and accurately
represented and thought

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.400
of when they use the product.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.520
Now I'll go into a little
bit of the research

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.920
that we've been doing over
the past year on the business

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.760
case for inclusion.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:15.390
I hope it's helpful
to start to frame what

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:18.240
users and consumers are thinking
about as it relates to products

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.030
inclusion, but also how you
can start to implement it

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:23.800
in your everyday work.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:27.900
So the first piece of data
that I wanted to share with you

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.870
all is that there are four
places in the product design

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:33.480
process where it's
really important

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.310
to bring that inclusive lens.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:39.000
Those four points are the
ideation, user research, user

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.070
testing, and marketing phases.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.080
Now a few slides
back, I showed you

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.720
an end-to-end product
inclusion process

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.470
slide, where you saw
multiple inflection points,

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.060
or points in the process
where you could bring product

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:52.980
inclusion into the fold.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:55.290
That being said, when we
did research and looked

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:58.710
at a bunch of different teams
across Google, what we found

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.290
was that these are the four
points where teams constantly

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:02.460
came back to.

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.050
And also, these
were the four points

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.240
where if you did bring
an inclusive lens

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.155
and were intentional
about doing so,

00:08:08.155 --> 00:08:09.780
you actually
disproportionately created

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:10.920
a more inclusive product.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.380
And so we encourage
you all to think about

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.305
if these four points
in the process

00:08:15.305 --> 00:08:16.680
make sense for
your organization,

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:18.972
and how you can bring that
inclusive lens to those four

00:08:18.972 --> 00:08:20.100
points.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.870
It's also important to just
start to ask, who else?

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:25.200
So, for example,
if you're starting

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.370
to ideate within
your organization,

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:30.330
maybe bring someone from another
piece of the organization

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.460
or another team into
the brainstorming

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.747
because they'll likely have
different perspectives.

00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:36.330
If you're already
doing user research,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.490
maybe you do user
research internationally

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:41.400
or with a different demographic
than you usually do.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:44.010
These small changes can
lead to really big results

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:48.400
as it relates to bringing
that inclusive lens.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.180
The second piece was
that 100% of teams

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.280
that brought an inclusive
lens into their process

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.090
did so at least two of the four
touch points I just mentioned.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:09:00.180
So either the ideation,
user research, user testing,

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.790
or marketing phases,
they did at least two.

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:06.420
So it may be the ideation and
marketing or the user research

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:07.680
and user testing.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.330
But they made sure
that it wasn't just

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.580
an isolated point in time,
but that they were doing it

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:12.538
throughout the process.

00:09:12.538 --> 00:09:14.400
And I think that that's
really important.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.830
Not only because you're
starting to think holistically

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:19.683
about your process, but also
you can involve different team

00:09:19.683 --> 00:09:21.600
members and different
organization members who

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.610
have different
expertise to, again,

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:26.580
start to bring more perspectives
that have historically been

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:30.480
underrepresented into the fold.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.300
Another key finding was
that both underrepresented

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.060
and majority groups prefer
inclusive marketing and product

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:36.930
design.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:38.220
What does that mean?

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.310
It's been a notion that only
underrepresented groups want

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:42.480
to see themselves reflected.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.480
And what we found is that
that's actually not true.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.490
Everyone, or the
majority of people,

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.800
want to see diversity
and inclusion,

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:51.460
both in marketing
and storytelling,

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:52.590
but also in product design.

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:54.450
And so I think that
that's a big shift

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.860
that we want to make
sure that we think about.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.400
It's not just those who
have been historically

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.140
underrepresented,
but it's actually

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:05.990
underrepresented in the
majority consumers or users.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.570
The last finding that I'd
like to share with you all

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:11.480
is that teams, no matter
their representation,

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:14.840
that were intentional about
bringing an inclusive lens

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:16.460
were able to do so.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.350
So regardless of the
team makeup-- and we

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:21.140
talk a lot about the
systemic changes that

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:25.130
need to happen in order to have
representation and inclusive

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.070
culture, that is
incredibly important still.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:32.210
But even as you're working to
get to that representation,

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:34.970
you can start to work on
product inclusion now.

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:38.450
So regardless of the makeup,
if you're setting standards,

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.670
if you're holding yourself
and your team accountable,

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:43.220
you can actually
start to make headway

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.363
in building that
inclusive product.

00:10:46.363 --> 00:10:48.530
So now I'm just going to
go through three principles

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:52.100
that we help to guide teams
along their product inclusion

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:54.018
journey.

00:10:54.018 --> 00:10:55.560
The first one is to
address the user.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.310
So I showed you a few
of the 12 dimensions

00:10:58.310 --> 00:11:01.970
that we focus on as it
relates to product inclusion.

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.280
But really, thinking
about what dimensions

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:07.190
have been underrepresented
in your process,

00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:08.690
and bringing those
into the fold.

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:10.070
You want to address the users.

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:11.690
Again, asking, who else?

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.820
And continuing to
expand the pool who uses

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.565
could and should be.

00:11:15.565 --> 00:11:17.190
The second one is to
start with equity.

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:19.080
So inclusion shouldn't
be an afterthought.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.830
It's not something that comes
in after you've actually

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:23.210
created everything.

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:25.590
How can you bring it in
earlier on in the process

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:27.710
and at multiple
points in the process?

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:29.450
Again, this doesn't
sit within one team.

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:32.280
It sits within all parts
of the organization.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:34.280
And so, how are you
making sure that you

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.490
found those key process points.

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:38.240
And you've brought the
key stakeholders in

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.790
to help move that along.

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.980
And the last one is
to continually test.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.830
So just like any other part of
your product design process,

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.580
this needs to be iterative.

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:51.270
You want to make sure that you
aren't just checking a box,

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:52.650
but you're constantly shifting.

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:53.817
You're constantly improving.

00:11:53.817 --> 00:11:55.483
You're constantly
getting feedback, just

00:11:55.483 --> 00:11:57.608
like any other part of your
product design process.

00:11:57.608 --> 00:11:59.400
You want to make sure
that you're learning,

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:02.010
you're growing, and you're
bringing more voices

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:04.260
into the fold, and
you're adapting

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.970
as you're getting feedback.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.760
So this, just like any
other part of your process,

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:09.680
you want to commit to.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:12.310
You want to have standards and
accountability frameworks for.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:16.270
And you want to constantly,
constantly be improving.

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:19.080
And so with that, thank you so
much for joining the product

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:20.680
inclusion session.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:24.130
I hope it was helpful,
and have a great day.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:25.970
Thanks so much.

